# =============================================================================
# DeepSeek-V4-Flash MXFP4 at 131072 -- kvram placement + MTP speculative decoding
# =============================================================================
# The generation-first sibling of `deepseek-v4-flash-128k-kvram`. Same lossless
# MXFP4 weights, same KV-in-RAM placement, plus the 5.5 GiB MTP predictor.
#
#   MEASURED at 32k depth, caches on (RESULTS §14.1):
#     kvram default (--n-cpu-moe 17)   499.2 pp / 21.26 tg
#     this profile  (n19 + MTP)        434.5 pp / 24.12 tg
#   i.e. +13.5 % generation for -13 % prefill.
#
# Why n19 and not n17: the predictor needs ~6 GiB of VRAM and the n17 placement
# has 1.6 GiB free, so it is paid for with two placement steps. Isolated, the
# placement change alone costs 1.23 tok/s (21.26 -> 20.03) and MTP then adds
# +20.4 % on top (20.03 -> 24.12). n20 is worse on both axes, so 19 is optimal.
#
# NOT a quality trade: speculative decoding verifies every drafted token against
# the target model, so output is what the target would have produced. That is
# what makes this worth having on the lossless path.
#
#   ./serve.sh deepseek-v4-flash-128k-kvram-mtp
#   ./serve-deepseek-v4-flash-mxfp4-kvram-mtp-128k.sh
#
# WHICH ONE TO RUN. Against the plain kvram default it is a straight swap of
# prefill for generation:
#   * long prompts, RAG, document work  -> `deepseek-v4-flash-128k-kvram`
#   * long answers, agents, chat        -> this profile
# For raw generation speed regardless of quant, the 2-bit
# `deepseek-v4-flash-mtp` profile still wins outright at 87-94 tok/s (§7).
#
# Requires the predictor (5.5 GiB, one-time download):
#   huggingface.co/philpax/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-MTP-Only-GGUF
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/lmstudio-community/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-GGUF/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-MXFP4-00001-of-*.gguf}"

: "${IK_ALIAS:=deepseek-v4-flash-128k-kvram-mtp}"
: "${IK_CTX:=131072}"

# Manual placement, one notch looser than the kvram default to house the draft.
: "${IK_FIT:=0}"
: "${IK_NCMOE:=19}"

# -rtr STAYS ON HERE, and this was tested rather than assumed (RESULTS §27.4).
# Converting to the gpu-experts principle -- experts computed on the GPU, see §21 --
# does not fit: the draft model holds 5.5 GB of weights plus its own KV cache
# (-cd 8192) in VRAM, so --n-cpu-moe 21 at -ub 8192 will not load and n20 at
# -ub 4096 loads and then dies at depth in cuMemCreate. Both CUDA OOM, not the NaN
# abort.
#
# It would be the wrong trade anyway. This profile measures 27.01 tg at 4k and
# 26.51 at 32k against 20.29 for the plain kvram profile -- +33 %, the highest
# generation recorded on this model, which is the entire reason it exists.
: "${IK_RTR:=1}"
: "${IK_UBATCH:=2048}"
: "${IK_BATCH:=4096}"
: "${IK_CTK:=f16}"
: "${IK_CTV:=f16}"

# The predictor. MTP is not baked into any DeepSeek-V4 quant -- they all strip
# the predictor tail -- so it is supplied separately (§7.2).
# -ngld 99 puts the draft on the GPU; ~1 GiB of it still lands on the host, which
# is expected and did not stop the +20 %.
: "${IK_MTP_DRAFT:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/philpax/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-MTP-Only-GGUF/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-MTP-bf16.gguf}"
: "${IK_EXTRA_ARGS:=-mla 3 -fidx --reasoning-format deepseek -nkvo -md ${IK_MTP_DRAFT} -ngld 99 -cd 8192 --spec-type mtp:n_max=1,p_min=0.0}"
